course: dictionary
lesson: 14317
title: Obligatory: must, or always expected
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Obligatory is a formal word that means you have to do something. It is not your choice. A rule, a law or a school says you must do it. «Wearing a seatbelt is obligatory in cars.» The rule says you must, so the seatbelt is not optional.
explain: Look for clues in the sentence. Words like rule, law, must, always or for all pupils are signs that obligatory is about a rule. «Reading the school rules is obligatory for all pupils.» Ask yourself: is somebody making me do this? If yes, obligatory fits.
explain: Writers also use obligatory in a playful way. «Grandad took the obligatory family photo at the picnic.» Nobody has a rule about this photo. It is a joke: everyone expects it to happen every time, so it always comes along.
fact: Obligatory means something you must do because a rule or law says so.
fact: «Attendance is obligatory» means you have to be there, and you cannot choose to stay away.
fact: Obligatory can also be a joke: «the obligatory selfie» is a photo that everyone expects and always takes.
fact: A good check is to swap in the word must. If «must» fits the sentence, obligatory probably fits too.
exercise: «Wearing a helmet is obligatory when you ride a bike.» What does obligatory mean here? | It only happens in summer | You can choose to do it if you like | *You must do it because a rule says so | It is a kind of bike
exercise: «Every year Mum takes the obligatory photo of us at the gate.» What does obligatory suggest? | A blurry photo by mistake | A secret photo | A photo nobody is allowed to take | *Something everyone expects, and it always happens
exercise: Find and copy the word that shows a rule says you must do it. «Swimming with a cap is obligatory at our pool.» | swimming | pool | cap | *obligatory
test: «Seatbelts are obligatory in cars.» What does obligatory mean? | It is only for fun | It is very expensive | *A rule says you must do it | You can choose it freely
test: «Grandma always gives the obligatory hug at the door.» What does obligatory suggest? | A hug that is rare and surprising | Something that is against the rules | A hug given by mistake | *Something expected that always happens
exam: Which meaning fits obligatory? | Something that is very old | *Something you must do because a rule or law says so | A kind of food | A choice you make freely
